  "answer_text": "Yes, the authors' conclusions about hallucination agree with their reported results. In the study on PopCultureQA, the authors conclude that \"Retrieval augmentation clearly reduces hallucinated content in the answers\" [S006, 0-1367]. The reported results support this conclusion, showing that the RAG pipeline has a hallucination rate of 18.3%, which is lower than the vanilla LLM's rate of 21.5% [S006, 1367-2401]. Similarly, in the study on HistoryQuest, the results show that the retrieval-augmented generator has a hallucination frequency of 5.1%, which is significantly lower than the no-retrieval baseline's 10.3% [S016, 1395-2667]. Although the specific conclusion text for the HistoryQuest study is not provided in the excerpts, the data aligns with the general finding that retrieval reduces hallucination rates compared to non-retrieval baselines.",
